# The input from which requirements.lock.txt (CPU) and requirements.lock.cu130.txt
# (GPU) are generated. It is NOT installed directly and NOT a lock.
#
# Why this exists, and why it is not requirements.txt: the model is the pinned
# upstream under third_party/mar, imported rather than copied, so this port's own
# files import only torch, numpy and PyYAML (those are requirements.txt). But the
# *environment* the pinned upstream needs to import and run is larger -- its
# model reads timm, scipy and tqdm, and torchvision comes with them -- and the
# lock must be a closure over that whole environment. Those extra packages are
# the pinned upstream's dependencies, taken from its environment.yaml (timm,
# scipy) and its `import` lines (tqdm, torchvision), and pinned here at versions
# compatible with this project's Python 3.12 / torch 2.13.0 rather than the
# upstream's Python 3.8 / torch 2.2 originals.
#
# Deliberately excluded, because step 1 does not use the upstream generation /
# FID / caching engine (engine_mar.py): opencv-python, tensorboard, gdown,
# torch-fidelity.
#
# Regenerate both locks with (from the repository root):
#
#   uv pip compile methods/mar/requirements.lock.in \
#       --constraint <a fleet CPU lock's name==version pins, hashes stripped> \
#       --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cpu \
#       --extra-index-url https://pypi.org/simple \
#       --python-version 3.12 --universal --generate-hashes \
#       -o methods/mar/requirements.lock.txt
#
# then the cu130 sibling per docs/GPU.md section 2.
torch
torchvision
numpy
PyYAML
timm
scipy
tqdm
